1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:35,415
Welcome to the executive briefing on Prism-A-N-N version three: a co-designed enterprise retrieval engine that delivers zero-leak, sub-millisecond vector search under real SpiceDB permissions. Across one hundred sixty nine thousand eight hundred ten audited queries on two public corpora and persistent PostgreSQL seventeen, Prism-A-N-N cuts authorization pre-filtering by two hundred seventy eight times, accelerates access revocation by two thousand six hundred sixty six times with zero vector writes, and achieves one hundred percent Recall at ten with zero security leaks.

2
00:00:36,215 --> 00:01:21,880
Why do enterprise retrieval systems struggle today? When hierarchical Zanzibar permissions meet H-N-S-W vector indexes, three severe bottlenecks emerge. First, streaming thousands of leaf document IDs from PostgreSQL-backed SpiceDB stalls queries for ninety eight milliseconds median before vector search even starts. Second, when a user can only view zero point one percent of a corpus, standard FAISS H-N-S-W collapses to five point seven five percent recall, while unbounded graph walks spike up to one hundred forty seven milliseconds. Third, storing permissions inline on vector rows causes massive write amplification: revoking one folder across one hundred fifty three thousand chunks stalls pgvector for nearly three full minutes.

3
00:01:22,680 --> 00:02:02,199
Prism-A-N-N solves all three bottlenecks by co-designing the authorization graph, the vector index, and the hardware scanner. In Layer One, Upward Boundary Pruning stamps immutable structural lineages at ingest and cuts SpiceDB traversal above leaf documents, splitting only around explicit deny walls. In Layer Two, Geometric Express Bridges add eight diverse semantic shortcuts per node across folders, parent containers, and Meaning Magnets for just one point nine percent extra graph memory. In Layer Three, Z-ordered Roaring bitmaps feed contiguous memory spans straight into a native C-plus-plus ARM NEON kernel with a multi-stage Local-Stall Guard.

4
00:02:02,999 --> 00:02:38,755
Our first confirmatory experiment tests live SpiceDB version one point fifty seven in both memory and durable PostgreSQL seventeen modes across twelve hundred principals with up to twenty deny walls. Upward Boundary Pruning matches SpiceDB with zero mismatches and obeys Theorem One in one hundred percent of cases. For broad principals accessing over one thousand documents, pre-filter compilation drops from ninety eight milliseconds on PostgreSQL SpiceDB down to zero point three five milliseconds locally, and three point four nine milliseconds even when making live gRPC calls.

5
00:02:39,555 --> 00:03:21,382
Next, we benchmarked seven live native engines across five hundred twenty two thousand BEIR Quora passages and two hundred fifty thousand Wikipedia passages. Our graph-only geometric bridges beat FAISS M equals sixteen by up to sixty one recall points, and beat compiled upstream C-plus-plus ACORN-1 and ACORN-gamma at every single selectivity on both corpora. When the native ARM NEON scanner engages at one percent selectivity and below, Prism-A-N-N hits one hundred percent recall in just zero point zero five to zero point eleven milliseconds — remaining five point five to forty one times faster than hnswlib and pgvector even after adding full PostgreSQL SpiceDB network latency.

6
00:03:22,182 --> 00:03:57,741
In real enterprise deployments, the hardest failure mode occurs when a user's permitted documents sit far from the query in embedding space. Under this negative correlation regime, FAISS collapses to five percent recall, and hnswlib takes up to forty five milliseconds. Prism-A-N-N's multi-stage Local-Stall Guard detects roughly ninety percent of stalled queries automatically and escalates them to ARM NEON Mode Three, restoring ninety seven point two percent recall on Quora and ninety six point four percent on Wikipedia in under zero point four milliseconds — over one hundred thirty times faster than hnswlib.

7
00:03:58,541 --> 00:04:33,255
Compliance teams care deeply about revocation speed: when an employee leaves a project or an ethical wall goes up, access must vanish immediately. Updating an inline permission column across one hundred fifty three thousand chunks in PostgreSQL pgvector triggers heavy heap rewrites and index maintenance, taking one hundred seventy nine seconds median. With Prism-A-N-N, revoking the exact same folder deletes a single edge in persistent PostgreSQL SpiceDB in sixty seven milliseconds with zero vector-store writes — two thousand six hundred sixty six times faster.

8
00:04:34,055 --> 00:05:03,429
To prove our gains are not an artifact of lucky entry points, we ran a strict four-way ablation on the exact same five hundred twenty two thousand node FAISS base graph. Adding four authorized entry portals to plain FAISS moves recall by less than one point. Conversely, removing portals completely from our geometric bridge graph retains over one hundred one percent of the recall lift across all sub-one-percent selectivities, proving decisively that Vamana-diverse intra-container bridges drive the connectivity win.

9
00:05:04,229 --> 00:05:39,231
Engineering credibility requires reporting every tradeoff transparently. An independent reviewer audited all raw logs and C-plus-plus kernels with zero prior context and awarded seven point five out of ten, accept, with zero blockers. We explicitly separate our one point nine percent graph-only memory footprint from the twenty four point eight percent total memory when keeping an auxiliary eight-bit quantized buffer alongside thirty-two-bit vectors, and we transparently separate Python mask marshaling overhead from pure kernel speed while showing that full end-to-end latency still dominates existing systems.

10
00:05:40,031 --> 00:06:09,660
To summarize our next steps: Prism-A-N-N version three is fully verified and ready for immediate arXiv submission following standard publication clearance, with all twelve pages, five vector figures, and one hundred sixty nine thousand raw audit records locked and reproducible. From here, we can pursue a top-tier database conference submission with full Pareto sweeps, or package the native C-plus-plus kernel as an enterprise pilot for regulated A-I knowledge platforms. Thank you for watching.
